<h1>CA Sea Change Atlas</h1>
<p class="lede">Two tools for making sense of California's sea-level-rise and coastal-flooding data: a live map of the flood-hazard layers that are actually open to reuse, and a side-by-side comparison of all 12 tools in this space.</p>
<p class="hero-about-link">Inspired by "Sea the Future," a state tool that ended when its funding ran out. <a href="{{ '/about.html' | url }}">Read more →</a></p>
</div>
</div>

<div class="main">
<div class="wrap">

<p class="landing-note">These aren't the same information rendered twice — pick based on what you need.</p>

<div class="landing-cards">
<div class="landing-card">
<h2>The map</h2>
<p>A Leaflet map with live flood-hazard data — currently BCDC's Bay Shoreline Flood Explorer — as checkable layers, address search, and a click-to-inspect popup showing real depth, acreage, and consequence values at a point. Use this when you want to see what a specific piece of coastline actually looks like under a given scenario.</p>
<a class="landing-card-cta" href="{{ '/map.html' | url }}">Open the map →</a>
</div>
<div class="landing-card">
<h2>Tool comparison</h2>
<p>A filterable grid of all 12 sea-level-rise and coastal-flooding tools California planners might reach for, plus a compare-up-to-three table covering scope, process, exposure, data, and known limitations. Use this when you're deciding which tool is the right one for your question in the first place.</p>
<a class="landing-card-cta" href="{{ '/sources.html' | url }}">Browse the 12 tools →</a>
</div>
